Here’s the current configuration for proj-auth with secret or private values redacted:

config/default.json  
```
{
  "serviceName": "proj-auth",
  "port": 4000,
  "logLevel": "info",

  "db": {
    "host": "db.internal",
    "port": 5432,
    "name": "auth_service",
    "user": "auth_user",
    "password": "••••••••"          // redacted
  },

  "jwt": {
    "issuer": "proj-auth",
    "audience": "proj-frontend",
    "expiresIn": "15m",
    "secret": "••••••••"           // redacted
  },

  "cors": {
    "allowedOrigins": [
      "https://app.example.com",
      "https://staging-app.example.com"
    ],
    "allowCredentials": true
  }
}
```

config/production.json  
```
{
  "logLevel": "warn",

  "db": {
    "host": "db-prod.internal",
    "password": "••••••••"          // redacted
  },

  "jwt": {
    "expiresIn": "10m",
    "secret": "••••••••"           // redacted
  }
}
```

config/staging.json  
```
{
  "logLevel": "debug",

  "db": {
    "host": "db-staging.internal",
    "password": "••••••••"          // redacted
  },

  "jwt": {
    "expiresIn": "5m",
    "secret": "••••••••"           // redacted
  }
}
```

Key items to note for your JWT debugging:

1. issuer / audience must match what the frontend is validating.  
2. The `expiresIn` is 15 mins (default), 10 mins (production), 5 mins (staging). Ensure the frontend’s token-refresh logic aligns with these.  
3. Verify that the frontend is using the correct environment (`production` vs `staging`) so it expects the right expiration.  
4. If you recently rotated the `jwt.secret`, be sure deployments of both services picked up the new value and caches were cleared.
